# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for rcc
#+STARTUP: overview
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil ^:nil
#+TODO: TODO WAITING | DONE

The queue, one chapter per part of the tree, in the order the work is
wanted; within a chapter, priority order top first. Each entry states
the fact, the fix and the check that settles it. A =WAITING= entry is
investigated and reported and waits on its owner; it leaves when the
fix it waits for lands and its check passes. Finished entries leave;
git keeps them.

#+TOC: headlines 2

* The back end

The direction of this branch. rcc drops LLVM's code generator and
writes x86-64 assembly itself; the system cc assembles, links and
preprocesses. The reasons are =docs/findings.md= and =docs/llvm.md=,
the design is =docs/backend.md= with its calls made in
=docs/design-calls.md=. LLVM stays where it benefits and is rewritten where
it has to be fought. The entries below are the slices, in order; the
chapters after this one hold the LLVM-era queue, and each says what
leaves with the pipeline.

** TODO Retire the LLVM pipeline

Every program of the corpus and of the cost corpus passes through
the new back end, so the old one can go: =emit.cc= and the flag table
of =main.cc= leave; =-S= writes assembly; =-c= runs the system cc on
it; =llc= is no longer run.
=cost.md= chapters 2, 4 and 5 leave with it, chapter 3 is the table
the back end was written from, =guarantees.md='s IR clauses become
instruction clauses, =shape.md= describes the new pipeline, and
=findings.md= closes with what became of its five. The header
comments of the cost programs and of the corpus programs that
describe the LLVM listings or the IR are rewritten for the native
ones; the pins of =tests/corpus/native/= and
=tests/cost/native/= move up a directory over the LLVM pins; the
=--native= mode leaves the two scripts, and =test-native= becomes
=test=.

Check: =make test= green with no =llc= on the machine's path.

** TODO Redo the measurement

=make bench= under the new back end. Compiled with =-native=,
=collatz-tuned= warns twice, for =x= and =steps=, and =matmul-tuned=
five times, for =pc=, =bcol=, =pa=, =pb= and =s=; those seven take
slots. =sieve-tuned=, =fib-tuned=, =sort-tuned= and =strings-tuned=
warn not at all: sieve declares six, but its two loops are sibling
blocks, so never more than five are live at once. The numbers move.
The movement is a result about the model, and the tuned sources may
be re-tuned to the set, as a PDP-11 programmer tuned to three.

Check: chapter 6 of =cost.md= re-recorded, with the register count
per program.

** TODO The sixth register's price is counted by declarations

=design-calls.md= 2.2 prices a sixth register from the =register=
variables each tuned =main= declares. But c0 counts the variables
live at once: a block's variables leave the count at its end. The
warnings follow the live count, so sieve, which declares six and
holds at most five live, gets none. Three sentences thus rest
on the wrong count:

- 2.2: "with five, sieve loses one too", where sieve loses none;
- 3.2: "four of six =main= functions exceed five", where two do by
  the live count, matmul and collatz, and three by declarations;
- 2.2, 2.3 and =backend.md= 13.1: the sixth register "was worth one
  variable in one program of six". By the live count it keeps one
  more variable out of a slot in each of matmul and collatz, and
  fits no program whole that five do not.

Fix: 2.2's table gains the most live at once per function, and the
sentences that price the sixth register are restated from it; 2.3's
conclusion is re-read against the restated price.

Check: no sentence of the two documents counts a sixth register
variable by declarations where the rule counts the live ones.

** TODO aarch64

A second instruction table and the AAPCS64 convention: =x0= to =x7=
for arguments, =x19= to =x28= callee-saved, ten register variables.
After x86-64 is whole.

* Guarantees

The promises in =docs/guarantees.md=. The entry number is the
guarantee it belongs to. All are decided; G15 is withdrawn with its
number reserved; under the literal pipeline the guard of G4, the
round trip of G8 and the laundering of G16 are no longer emitted,
because nothing between the source and the instruction reasons about
the program.

** TODO G7's "Lost" in the rationale misses the equality fold

=rationale.md= says G7 loses "nothing beyond G6". The corpus says
otherwise: =clang -O2= folds =&a + 1 == &b= to false for two distinct
locals, the one-past-the-end address included, where rcc compares the
addresses and finds them equal (=tests/corpus/g7-ptr-equal.c=, 1
under rcc, 0 under clang). A relational compare of the two is not
folded.

Fix: the Lost clause of G7 names the equality fold between distinct
objects, with the corpus program as its probe.

Check: the rationale's G7 names the fold and the program.

** TODO The rationale has no G17

=guarantees.md= has G17, the writable string literal, and its row in
the never-emitted table; =rationale.md= has neither, so the promise
has no recorded argument, alternatives or probe. The corpus has the
probe: =clang -O2= folds the load from a literal to its initializer
and drops the store (=tests/corpus/g17-string-store.c=, 122 under
rcc, 97 under clang).

Fix: a Storage section in the rationale with G17: the ISO paragraph,
the promise, the IR, what is lost (equal literals merged, the
read-only section, the fold of a load from a literal), and the probe.

Check: the rationale has a G17 entry and the table row.

* Cost model

The record in =docs/cost.md=: what each construct compiles to under
the literal pipeline, =llc= with FastISel and the rewriting passes
off. The entries below the first are the findings of the measurement
(=docs/findings.md=); they are facts about LLVM's code generator and
leave with it when the pipeline is retired. Under the back end of
=docs/backend.md= a multiply is =imul=, a constant is an immediate, a
register variable is a register, and a negative constant is folded in
the parser.

** TODO A function whose every path returns still gets the indeterminate slot

=needs_return_slot= allocates =%ret.indeterminate= in every non-void
function but =main=, for the value a fall off the end returns (G5).
A function whose last top-level statement is a =return= cannot fall
off the end, so its slot is a dead =alloca=, eight bytes of frame per
such function.

Fix: no slot when the body's last statement is a =return=; the
syntactic test is enough, since the slot is read only by the fall-off
path =finish= writes.

Check: =int f(void) { return 1; }= has no =ret.indeterminate= in its
IR; a function ending in an =if= without =else= still has one.

** TODO The selector rewrites a multiply and an unsigned division by a power of two

FastISel turns a =mul= by a power of two into =shl=, a =udiv= by one
into =shr= and a =urem= by one into =and= (=FastISel::fastEmit_ri_=
and =FastISel::selectBinaryOp=, =FastISel.cpp=); no option gates it.
So =x * 4= is =shl $0x2= for any =x=, and for an unsigned =x=, =x /
4= is =shr $0x2= and =x % 4= is =and $0x3=. A signed division or
remainder by a power of two stays =cltd; idiv=, as =div-four= pins.
Chapter 1 says =x / 4= is a division, and chapter 3 gives =imul= and
=div= for every operand; neither holds for these three, and no cost
row covers them. =tests/perf/collatz.c= declares its value signed so
that its halving divides.

Fix: decide between the emitter freezing a power-of-two constant
operand of =mul=, =udiv= and =urem=, as it freezes the constant of a
compare, so the instruction written is selected, and recording the
rewrite in chapter 4 as the selector's own rule, with chapters 1 and
3 qualified.

Check: cost rows for =x * 4=, unsigned =x / 4= and unsigned =x % 4=
pin =imul=, =div= and =div=, or pin the rewrite chapter 4 records.

** TODO A pass with no switch drops a constant already in its register

=machine-latecleanup=, after register allocation, removes an
instruction that loads an immediate into a register already holding
that immediate on every path to it; =llc= 23.1.3 has no option to
disable it. In =tests/perf/collatz.c=, =x % 2= loads 2 into =rcx= for
its =idiv=, and =x / 2= in the next block divides by that =rcx= with
no =mov= of its own: =llc -print-after-all= shows two =MOV32ri64 2=
up to that pass and one after it. Chapter 2 names the machine
combiner as the one pass without a switch, and chapter 1 says the
same expression written twice is computed twice.

Fix: name the pass in chapter 2 and its effect in chapter 4, with a
cost row of one constant used in two blocks, the first dominating
the second.

Check: the row's pin shows one =mov= of the constant, and chapter 2
names both passes.

** TODO Chapter 3 has no row for unary minus and complement

Chapter 3 has no row for =-a= or =~a=. A negative constant is not
one of them: the parser folds =-1= to a constant, so =long x = -1= is
one store of an immediate, as =long x = 2= is, and =p[-1]= is a
displacement. On a variable the two operators are instructions.
Under the LLVM pipeline =-a= is =sub 0, a=, which FastISel selects as
an =xor= that clears a second register and a =sub= from it, and =~a=
is =xor $0xffffffff=; under =-native= they are =neg= and =not=, the
row of =backend.md= 6.3.

Fix: a row for =-a= and =~a=, with a cost program. Chapter 3 is the
table that stays when the pipeline is retired, so the program is
worth its native pin; its LLVM pin shows the two-instruction negation
and leaves with the pipeline.

Check: the row and its pins.

** TODO A register pointer to a local array is a lea at each use

A local array's address is a frame index to the selector, and a
=register= variable initialized with it is that address itself, since
a register variable is its SSA value. The selector materializes it
with a =lea= from =rsp= at each use, inside a loop too: in a loop,
=p != first= with =register int *first = a;= is a =lea= and a =cmp=,
the same as =p != a=. Chapter 3's row for a =register= local says "a
register".

Fix: the =register= row of chapter 3 says that a register variable
holding a local's address is a =lea= at each use, with a cost
program.

Check: the row and its pin.

** TODO The flag tables name two rows that no longer witness their flag

=llvm.md= 3.5 and =cost.md= chapter 5 map each =llc= flag to the cost
rows whose pins change when the flag is dropped. They name =invariant=
under =-disable-machine-licm= and =twice= under
=-disable-machine-cse=. The parser now folds =12 * 34= to 408, so
neither program holds a constant instruction for machine LICM to move
out of the loop or for machine CSE to merge; dropping either flag
leaves both pins as they are (=llc= 23.1.4, probed outside the tree).
Each flag keeps one witness: =constant-in-loop='s =movabs= for LICM,
and for CSE =zero-test='s =never()=, whose =if (0)= is a =cmp= of two
registers each cleared by an =xor=. The two programs' header comments
already say they have no witness; the tables do not. This leaves with
the pipeline.

Fix: the two tables drop =invariant= and =twice=, or each program
regains a witness with a constant the selector must hold in a
register: a constant divisor, since =idiv= takes no immediate.

Check: dropping each flag changes every row the two tables name for
it.

** TODO The LLVM-era documents describe the unfolded constants

The parser folds integer constant expressions, so a negative constant
is an immediate, an array size is any integer constant expression,
and a comparison or a =!= of integer constants is a constant. Three
documents of the LLVM pipeline still describe the parser before the
evaluator, and leave or are rewritten with the pipeline:

- =cost.md= chapter 3: the =a < b= row freezes "a constant" among
  the operands of one name, but the parser folds a comparison of two
  integer constants, so only two pointer constants, =(int *) 0 ==
  (int *) 0=, still reach the row; the implicit-conversion row says
  =(long) 2= is the =(T) a= row, where it is now a =long= constant.
- =cost.md= chapter 4, the compare fold: "=if (0)= and =!0= compare
  0 with a frozen 0"; =!0= is now the constant 1, as =zero-test=
  pins.
- =findings.md= chapter 0: a negative constant "is computed at run
  time", and an array size "must be a single integer token".
- =findings.md= 4.2: the IR =sub i32 0, 1= and the listings'
  =xor=, =sub=, =cltq= are the parser's old output.
- =findings.md= 4.3: the constant row "is true of =2= and false of
  =-1="; it is now true of both.
- =findings.md= 4.4: "the operators of 6.6p8", where 6.6p3 restricts
  the operators and p8 lists the operands; and the evaluator
  "diagnoses [overflow] rather than wrapping", where it wraps and
  refuses the wrapped value only where 6.6p4 governs, the array size.
- =findings.md= 5.2: the message "not yet: array size other than an
  integer constant", which the parser no longer writes.
- =findings.md= chapter 7: "The parser does no constant evaluation".
- =findings.md= chapter 8: findings 4 and 5 "land together with the
  evaluator", which has landed.
- =llvm.md= 5.4: "=!0= to a constant", a fold of an =icmp= the
  emitter no longer writes for =!0=.

Fix: at retirement, when =findings.md= closes with what became of its
five and =cost.md='s chapters 2, 4 and 5 leave; =findings.md= 4.4's
citation is corrected then too.

Check: no sentence of the three documents names an unfolded constant.

* Tests

** TODO The oracle's time limit cannot tell 124 from a timeout

GNU =timeout= exits 124 for a run it stopped and passes the program's
own status otherwise, so a program that exits 124 by itself is
recorded as =timeout=. And =timeout= sends SIGTERM, which a program
may ignore; =timeout -k= would follow with SIGKILL.

Fix: =timeout -k 1 10=, and a corpus rule that no program exits 124.

Check: a program that ignores SIGTERM ends its row.

** TODO A signal death records as 128 + n, like an exit with 128 + n

A program killed by signal n is recorded as 128 + n: =timeout= dies
of the child's signal, and the shell reports 128 + n. So the G9
program's SIGSEGV is the row's 139, and a program that exits 139 by
itself records the same.

Fix: show a status above 128 by its signal name, =SEGV= from =kill
-l=, in the table and in =expected=, and a corpus rule that no
program exits above 128.

Check: the =g9-null-load= row reads =SEGV= and passes.

** TODO A signal death leaves a core file in /tmp

The oracle runs each program with the caller's core limit. Where it is
unlimited and =core_pattern= is =/tmp/core.%e.%p.%s=, as on this
machine, every run of =g9-null-load= under rcc leaves a core of about
290 KB in =/tmp=, and =timeout= says "the monitored command dumped
core" in the log.

Fix: =ulimit -c 0= in the script before the runs.

Check: =make test= leaves no new =/tmp/core.*=.

** TODO A G14 corpus program needs a non-constant condition

=for (;;);= hangs under =clang -O2= as well: C11 6.8.5p6 exempts a
loop whose controlling expression is constant from the
forward-progress assumption. The divergence G14 promises shows only
on a loop like =while (x);= with =x= a non-volatile object the loop
never writes.

Fix: write that program as the G14 entry, expected =timeout= under
rcc and 0 under clang.

Check: the oracle row.

** TODO The register rows pin only part of c1's register rules

The five programs that declare =register= pin the registers in
declaration order, the pushes with their unwind offsets, a register
parameter's move from its argument register, a read as an operand, a
copy before an instruction that writes, an assignment, a constant's
immediate, and G5's unwritten register. No program of either corpus
shows the rest of =backend.md= chapter 4, so a change to any of these
shows in no pin:

- a compound assignment or a =++= on the register in place, =addl $1,
  %r13d=;
- a narrow register variable: the extending store =movsbl %al,
  %ebx=, the re-extension after a step, and an unwritten one's
  extension at its declaration;
- the copy of a held read before a write, =i + i++=, and before the
  paths of =&&=, =||= and =?:= (G13);
- a register parameter past the sixth, loaded from its stack slot,
  and the order of the moves and the stores among mixed parameters;
- the epilogue's =leaq -8k(%rbp), %rsp= before the pops, which only a
  function with pushes and a =subq= writes;
- the sixth live variable in a slot, and a later block taking =rbx=
  again.

Fix: a cost program for each, with its two pins until retirement.

Check: perturbing each rule in =c1.cc= turns a row red.

** TODO =-o -= names a file, not standard output

=rcc -S -o -= writes a file called =-= in the working directory. Every
cc reads =-o -= as standard output for =-S=, and a reader of the IR
expects it.

Fix: with =-S=, an output of =-= writes the text to standard output;
with =-c= or a link it stays a usage error.

Check: =rcc -S -o - x.c | head -1= prints the first line of the IR
and leaves no file.

** TODO A failed =llc= under =-c= may leave a partial object

With =-c=, =llc= writes straight to the output path. If it fails
after opening the file, the path holds a partial object that a
later =make= would take for a good one. Unchecked.

Fix: write to a temporary beside the output and rename on success,
or confirm =llc= removes its output on failure.

Check: a fake =llc= that writes then exits 7 leaves no output file.

* Emitter

The IR emitter leaves with the LLVM pipeline. The three entries below
leave with it: the first two are properties of the IR, and the third,
the join of register variables, has no counterpart when a register
variable is a fixed register.

** TODO The object names the temporary IR file, so no two builds match

The IR has no =source_filename=, so =llc= names the module after the
file it reads, the driver's temporary =rcc-XXXXXX.ll=. The object's
file symbol is that name (=objdump -t= shows =rcc-BRXkWH.ll=), and two
=rcc -c= builds of the same source differ in it.

Fix: the emitter writes ~source_filename = "<the .c path>"~ at the
top of the IR.

Check: two =rcc -c= builds of =tests/cost/local.c= compare equal with
=cmp=, and =objdump -t= names the =.c= file.

** TODO A loop's end block with several predecessors

When =break= and =continue= arrive, a loop's end block gets several
predecessors: the two-arm join of the register variables generalizes
to n arms then.

Fix: =join= over a list of arms.

Check: a register variable assigned before a =break= has that value
after the loop.

* Parser

** TODO A warning recorded before an error is never printed

The parser records a warning in the unit, and the driver prints the
unit's warnings only after a successful parse. =error_t= ends the
parse at the first error, so the unit and its warnings are lost with
the exception. Thus =&k= on a sixth register variable reports the
error alone: the warning that put =k= in a slot was recorded at its
declaration, before the error, and is never printed.

Fix: the driver prints the warnings the parser recorded before the
error too. Since the unit is lost with the exception, the warnings
need a home the exception does not destroy, such as a list the
driver passes to the parser.

Check: =int f(void) { register int a, b, c, d, e, k; return *&k; }=
prints the warning at =k= and then the error at =&=, exit status 1.

** TODO Assignment to a const object is not diagnosed

The parser accepts =const= and drops it: the type is the machine's
view and carries no qualifier. So =const int c = 1; c = 2;= compiles,
though 6.5.17.1p2 requires a modifiable lvalue and 6.3.2.1p1 excludes
a const-qualified one; a conforming compiler must diagnose it. The
same silence covers a =const= pointee assigned through, and a
=T *= initialized from a =const T *=.

Fix: qualifiers on the symbol and on the pointee, checked by
=require_modifiable_lvalue= and by the assignment conversion; the
type itself stays unqualified.

Check: the three programs above fail with a diagnostic; =const= in
every other position still parses.

** TODO GNU extensions in system headers reach the parser as identifiers

`clang -E` of a program that includes `stdio.h` or most other glibc
headers leaves `__attribute__`, `__extension__`, `__restrict`,
`__inline`, `__asm__` and their arguments in the text; the lexer
hands them over as identifiers and the parser refuses the first.
`limits.h` has none, so the first slice does not meet them. Any
corpus program that calls into libc will. The same family: `_Bool`,
`bool`, `inline` and every keyword outside =tok_t= arrive as
identifiers and fail as undeclared names rather than as `not yet`.

Fix: decide between skipping the extensions in the parser (an
attribute and its parenthesized argument list dropped, the others
dropped as words) and preprocessing with a flag set that hides them.
A probe says which headers need what.

Check: `#include <stdio.h>` followed by an empty `main` parses.

** TODO A parameter's array size cannot name an earlier parameter

=int f(int n, int a[n])= fails with "'n' undeclared". Before the
evaluator, any size but one integer token was a not-yet; the size is
now parsed as an expression, and the name in it is looked up. The
parser declares a definition's parameters only after the whole
declarator, in =function_definition=, and a prototype's never, so no
parameter is in scope inside the list. The program is valid C: =n='s
scope begins just after its declarator (6.2.1p7), and a definition's
parameter list has block scope (6.2.1p4). The size changes nothing:
6.7.7.4p6 adjusts the parameter to a pointer, and at prototype scope
6.7.7.3p5 treats a size that is not an integer constant expression as
=*=.

Fix: a scope opened at the parameter list, each parameter declared as
its declarator completes, which a definition keeps as its body's
scope; a parameter's size that is not an integer constant expression
taken as =*=. In a definition this skips the evaluation of the size
that 6.7.7.3p5 asks for, which only a size with a side effect shows.

Check: =int f(int n, int a[n]) { return a[0]; }= compiles, and
under =-native= to the listing of =int f(int n, int *a)=; =int g(int
n) { int a[n]; }= is still the variable-length not-yet.

** TODO A =(void *) 0= arm makes =?:= a =void *=

=c ? (void *) 0 : p= with =p= an =int *= has type =void *= in the
tree, so =*(c ? (void *) 0 : p)= fails with "indirection through a
pointer to void". 6.5.16p7 gives the result the type of the other
operand when one operand is a null pointer constant, and =(void *) 0=
is one (6.3.2.3p3); the result is an =int *=. =common_pointer()=
asks whether an operand is a null pointer constant only when it is
not a pointer. Two pointers of different types thus meet at =void *=
whenever one is a =void *=, which is p7's rule for a pointer to void
that is not a null pointer constant.

Fix: in =common_pointer()=, a null pointer constant takes the other
operand's type before the rule for =void *= applies.

Check: =int f(int c, int *p) { return *(c ? (void *) 0 : p); }=
compiles; =c ? v : p= with =v= a =void *= variable is still =void *=.

** TODO The operand of =!= is not promoted

=!E= is defined as =(0==E)= (6.5.4.3p5), and the equality operator
promotes a =char= operand to =int=. The parser builds =!c= on the
unpromoted =char=, and c1 compares it at the =char='s width, =cmpb
$0=. The result is the same, 0 or 1, but the tree does not say what
the standard says, and the listing shows a byte compare where the
operation is on an =int=.

Fix: the parser inserts the promotion cast on the operand of =!=; c1
then writes =cmpl=.

Check: the listing of =!c= on a =char= under =-native= is the load
=movsbl= and =cmpl $0=.

** TODO A shift count is widened to the shifted operand's type

=shift()= converts the promoted count to the type of the promoted
left operand, because an IR shift takes one type for both. 6.5.8p3
promotes each operand on its own and asks nothing more. Under
=-native= the cast is an instruction: in =g6-ptr-wrap=, =ones >> (argc
+ 2)= on an =unsigned long= writes =movslq %ecx, %rcx= before =shrq
%cl, %rax=, and the shift reads =cl= alone. The row of =backend.md=
6.3 for =a << b= names no extension of the count.

Fix: at retirement, the count keeps its promoted type, and c1 moves it
to =rcx= at its own width; until then the extension is the cost of the
IR's shift, and the 6.3 row says so.

Check: =g6-ptr-wrap='s native pin has no =movslq= before =shrq=.

* Lexer

